<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Altenburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Altenburg is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>. An imperial castle and town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, it was the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pleissenland">Pleissenland</a>, the imperial territory later absorbed by the Wettins. From 1603 it was the residence of the dukes of Saxe-Altenburg, and it is known as the place where the card game <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skat">Skat</a> was developed in the early 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/altenburg.jpg" alt="Altenburg" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Friese · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altenburg_Frauenfels.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Altenburg lies in the flat, fertile landscape of Osterland on the Pleiße river, in the far east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> next to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, 40 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>. It is the capital of the Altenburger Land district. The city centre stands at about 200 m, on the Blaue Flut, a small tributary of the Pleiße that feeds the Großer Teich, an inner-city pond. Loess soil makes the surrounding land very fertile, and lignite is mined on the surface north of the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town was first mentioned in 976 in a deed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeitz">Zeitz</a>. Remains of a Slavic castle on the Schloßberg suggest a Slavic foundation, the capital of the shire of Plisni, taken over when Henry I conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>. Its position on the imperial road Via Imperii between Halle and Cheb gave it importance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-trade">salt trade</a>. The castle became an imperial palatinate and played an important part in the German settlement of the lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>. In the middle of the 12th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> emperors made Altenburg a Kaiserpfalz, and it became a market and a mint. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> built much there, visiting between 1165 and 1188, and the parish church was finished in 1172.</p><p>During the Interregnum of 1254 to 1273 the Terra Plisnensis was impounded, then bought back by Rudolph I. After the Battle of Lucka in 1307 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-i-of-belgium">King Albert I</a> lost Altenburg and the Pleiße lands to the Wettin margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, who held the city until 1918. Since the 17th century it was the residence of Ernestine duchies, among them Saxe-Altenburg, which lasted until the end of monarchy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1918. Industrialization came early in the 19th century, and the town became known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sewing-machine">sewing machines</a> and playing cards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The card game <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skat">Skat</a> is said to have originated in Altenburg in the 1810s, based on the Bavarian tarock, and the town is nicknamed the playing-card town. The Altenburger Spielkartenfabrik was founded in 1831. Friedrich Arnold Brockhaus, founder of the Brockhaus Enzyklopädie, lived and worked there from 1810 to 1817. The castle, with its 11th-century Mantelturm and a playing card museum, the Lindenau-Museum, founded in 1876, and the Gothic St. Bartholomew's Church, built between 1428 and 1443, are among the sights. The city has about 33,000 inhabitants and was left unscathed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, so its historic buildings are preserved.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Altenburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke Frederick of Saxe-Hildburghausen gave up his old duchy and received <b>Altenburg</b>, founding the new Duchy of Saxe-Altenburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-altenburg">Duchy of Saxe-Altenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>After a dispute among his relatives, Saxe-Eisenberg passed to Saxe-Gotha-Altenburg; in 1826 it went with <b>Altenburg</b> to the new Duchy of Saxe-Altenburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-eisenberg">Duchy of Saxe-Eisenberg</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1672 he inherited Saxe-<b>Altenburg</b>, and the state became known as Saxe-Gotha-Altenburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-gotha">Duchy of Saxe-Gotha</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke Frederick exchanged Hildburghausen for <b>Altenburg</b>, becoming Duke of Saxe-Altenburg, and Hildburghausen went to Saxe-Meiningen.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-hildburghausen">Duchy of Saxe-Hildburghausen</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1672, after a further rearrangement following the extinction of Saxe-<b>Altenburg</b>, Bernhard received Jena and its surroundings as the Duchy of Saxe-Jena.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-jena">Duchy of Saxe-Jena</a></li><li><blockquote>On 1 May 1920 Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach, Saxe-Meiningen, Saxe-<b>Altenburg</b>, Saxe-Gotha, Schwarzburg-Rudolstadt, Schwarzburg-Sondershausen and the People's State of Reuss merged into the Land of Thuringia, with Weimar as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-thuringia">Free State of Thuringia</a></li></ul></section>
